<article class="env-lemma" id="0AUQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AUQ">Lemma <span data-tag="0AUQ">10.120.15</span>.</a> A PID is a Dedekind domain. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R$ be a PID. Since every nonzero ideal of $R$ is principal, and $R$ is a UFD (Lemma <a href="/tag/034V" data-tag="034V">10.120.13</a>), this follows from the fact that every irreducible element in $R$ is prime (Lemma <a href="/tag/034T" data-tag="034T">10.120.5</a>) so that factorizations of elements turn into factorizations into primes. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>